# Tutorial 23 — Particles and Audio Visualizers
# Reference: docs/scripting/vns/language/vns-commands.md
# Read scripts/tutorial/README.md for the complete course and linked references.
@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_23
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[set ui.textBoxAsset none]
[set ui.continueIndicator spinning_arrow]
[if tutorial_still]
[set ui.reduceMotion true]
[else]
[set ui.reduceMotion false]
[endif]
[if !tutorial_initialized]
[set tutorial_count 0]
[flag tutorial_initialized]
[endif]
[set ui.characterHeightFactor 1.5]
[set ui.characterBaselineY 1.5]
[set character.lavender.imageEffect none]
{{BG_START}}
[textspeed 28]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Particles add weather and atmosphere. A visualizer turns audio analysis into motion. Both should support a scene without obscuring its text.
@label lesson_menu
> Demonstrate a short particle effect -> particles
> Demonstrate the music visualizer -> audio
> Tune and clean up effects -> practice
> Checkpoint -> checkpoint
> Finish and return to the hub -> return_hub
@label particles
[particles sakura intensity=0.25 opacity=0.65 speed=0.5 wind=5 duration=2500]
Lavender: These petals are a temporary effect over our white stage. Intensity, speed, size, wind, opacity, and layer each control a different part of the result.
[particles stop]
Lavender: Stop ends emission. Existing particles finish their fade; it is not an instant removal of every particle.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label audio
{{BGM_START}}
[visualizer on bars=24]
[visualizer set color=#8b72b0]
Lavender: With the bundled music enabled, the bars respond to sound. If this project omitted demo audio, add a local music file before trying the visualizer.
[visualizer off]
{{BGM_FADE}}
[jump lesson_menu]
@label practice
Lavender: Snow, rain, sakura, fireflies, dust, and leaves are preset starting points. Texture and tint options let you supply your own visual language.
Lavender: Continuous effects need explicit cleanup at scene exits. Timed effects need a duration that still makes sense when the player skips text.
Lavender: Exercise: compare a sparse effect behind the actors with one in front. Test slow reading, skip, pause, rollback, and a low-powered machine.
Lavender: Keep a still presentation available. Movement should never be the only way to learn a required fact.
[jump lesson_menu]
@label checkpoint
Lavender: Does stopping emission necessarily erase all existing particles immediately?
> No, they finish their normal fade -> correct
> Yes, every particle vanishes at once -> retry
@label retry
Lavender: Stop prevents new emission; the existing particles can finish naturally.
[jump checkpoint]
@label correct
Lavender: Right. Design your transition with that lifetime in mind.
[jump return_hub]

@label return_hub
[particles stop]
[visualizer off]
[if !tutorial_done_23]
[flag tutorial_done_23]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_5]
